ross the entire period. The zero-Jaccard edge diffs come from rotation of which group member is active, not from replacement of contacts, and the large short-bandwidth turnover spikes at t~22.5k-25.7k fall inside a gap with zero group events, i.e. a sparsity artifact. No abrupt old-set-to-new-set transition is present; only gradual background turnover."}, "raw_final_response": "**Analysis summary**\n\n- The group's contact set is essentially *the same ~30 external nodes* (65, 66, 74, 163, 232, 245, 292, 389, 422, 442, 526, 702, 848, 850, 880, 978, 986, 987, 990, 1200, 1317, 1358, 1456, 1566, 1587, 1633, 1681, 1780, 1888, 1999) throughout the whole period \u2014 verified individually for 1244, 1036, 1729 (steps 05, 13, 14), each with ~30\u201332 partners over the full 30k window.\n- Turnover at the requested half-window of 5000 never exceeds ~0.30, and that maximum sits at the very left boundary (t=14140, edge effect); mid-period values hover at 0.09\u20130.22 with no step (steps 01, 15). Before/after contact sets at the \"peak\" differ by only a handful of low-degree nodes.\n- The edge-level `diff_snapshots` Jaccard of 0.0 (steps 03, 08) is *not* evidence of a regime switch: the removed and added edges point to the **same** partner list; only *which group member* is emitting them rotates (25/396 early \u2192 638/489 \u2192 1244/1366 \u2192 1489/1729). Confirmed by per-node degree timeseries (step 09), where individual members switch on/off while the group's partner set is unchanged.\n- The two sharp turnover spikes at bandwidth 2500 (t\u224822537\u201325742, change 0.6\u20130.73, step 10) coincide with an activity void \u2014 bin [23140, 25139] has **0** group events (step 02) and the group's volume dips there (step 12) \u2014 so they are sparsity artifacts, not a contact-set replacement.\n- Volume is otherwise flat (~68\u201387 events per 2k bin, step 02) and group members are uniformly active (90\u201397 events each, step 07), so neither a volume burst nor popularity concentration explains anything; partner nodes are also modest-degree (step 11).\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_25544f86\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [25, 396, 489, 638, 1036, 1163, 1244, 1366, 1417, 1489, 1729, 1763],\n  \"window\": [9140, 39140],\n  \"confidence\": 0.78,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_15\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_05\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_13\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_14\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_09\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_10\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"With a 5000-unit half-window, the group's contact set before and after every candidate change point is nearly identical (turnover 0.09-0.30, maximum only at the window boundary). Every group member interacts with the same stable core of ~30 external partners across the entire period. The zero-Jaccard edge diffs come from rotation of which group member is active, not from replacement of contacts, and the large short-bandwidth turnover spikes at t~22.5k-25.7k fall inside a gap with zero group events, i.e. a sparsity artifact. No abrupt old-set-to-new-set transition is present; only gradual background turnover.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 85676, "total_output_tokens": 8201, "total_tokens": 93877, "total_latency_ms": 123840, "llm_calls": 9, "schema_repairs": 0, "status": "succ